    # Section 1: Introduction and General Guidelines
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 1. Introduction, Indications, Patient Safety and Sedation</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Indications for TEE')
    doc.append('TEE is indicated whenever transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) is technically limited or inconclusive, or when high-resolution imaging will alter clinical management:')
    doc.append('- Evaluation of specific cardiac sources of embolism (LAA thrombus, patent foramen ovale, mobile aortic atheroma).')
    doc.append('- Native and prosthetic valve infective endocarditis (vegetations, perivalvular abscess, fistula, leaflet perforation).')
    doc.append('- Evaluation of prosthetic heart valve dysfunction (paravalvular regurgitation, valve thrombosis, pannus ingrowth, structural deterioration).')
    doc.append('- Precise mechanism and quantitative severity of valvular regurgitation (repairability assessment).')
    doc.append('- Thoracic aortic dissection, transection, intramural hematoma, and penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer.')
    doc.append('- Real-time intraoperative guidance during cardiac surgery, transcatheter structural heart interventions (TAVR, MitraClip, Watchman LAA closure, ASD/PFO closure), and in critical care resuscitation.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 6: Absolute and Relative Contraindications to Transesophageal Echocardiography')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Category | Specific Pathological Conditions |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Absolute Contraindications** | • Perforated or torn viscus (esophagus, stomach).<br>• Active, uncontrolled upper gastrointestinal bleeding.<br>• Esophageal stricture, severe web, or obstructing esophageal mass/tumor.<br>• Esophageal diverticulum (e.g., Zenker diverticulum).<br>• Recent esophageal trauma, surgery, or anastomosis ($< 4	ext{--}6\text{ weeks}$). |')
    doc.append('| **Relative Contraindications** | • History of esophageal radiation therapy or previous esophagectomy.<br>• Known esophageal varices (Grade III/IV) or active portal hypertension (careful weighing of benefit vs risk).<br>• Severe Barrett\'s esophagus or active severe esophagitis.<br>• Unstable cervical spine injury or severe atlantoaxial subluxation.<br>• Coagulopathy or severe thrombocytopenia (platelets $< 50,000/\mu\text{L}$). |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 7: Complications Associated with Transesophageal Echocardiography')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Complication | Reported Incidence | Risk Factors & Prevention Strategies |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :---: | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Overall Major Complications** | $0.2	ext{--}0.5\%$ | Advanced age, uncooperative patient, altered anatomy, prior radiation. |')
    doc.append('| **Esophageal Perforation** | $0.01	ext{--}0.03\%$ | Catastrophic complication; occurs during blind advancement against resistance; *never force probe insertion*. |')
    doc.append('| **Major Gastrointestinal Bleeding** | $0.03	ext{--}0.1\%$ | Pre-existing varices, mucosal laceration, antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy. |')
    doc.append('| **Transient Hypopharyngeal Trauma / Sore Throat** | $0.1	ext{--}1.0\%$ | Multiple insertion attempts, excessive probe pressure, prolonged exam duration. |')
    doc.append('| **Hypoxemia, Laryngospasm, Bronchospasm** | $0.2	ext{--}0.5\%$ | Over-sedation, aspiration; continuous pulse oximetry and capnography required. |')
    doc.append('| **Transient Cardiac Arrhythmias (AF, SVT, PVCs)** | $0.5	ext{--}2.5\%$ | Vagal stimulation during probe passage, hypoxia, underlying heart disease. |')
    doc.append('| **Procedure-Related Mortality** | $< 0.01\%$ (1 in 10,000) | Typically secondary to unrecognized esophageal perforation or massive aspiration. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Patient Sedation and Recovery')
    doc.append('Non-intubated patients require topical pharyngeal anesthesia (lidocaine spray) and titrated conscious sedation (midazolam and fentanyl). Patients must be classified pre-procedure according to the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) Physical Status classification (Table 8) and monitored continuously. Post-procedure discharge requires a Modified Aldrete Score $\ge 9$ (Table 9).')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 8: ASA Physical Status Classification')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| ASA Class | Definition & Patient Status |')
    doc.append('| :---: | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Class I** | Normal healthy patient without systemic disease. |')
    doc.append('| **Class II** | Patient with mild systemic disease without functional limitation (e.g., well-controlled hypertension, mild asthma). |')
    doc.append('| **Class III** | Patient with severe systemic disease with substantive functional limitation (e.g., stable angina, COPD, EF 35–49%). |')
    doc.append('| **Class IV** | Patient with severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life (e.g., unstable angina, decompensated heart failure, severe AS). |')
    doc.append('| **Class V** | Moribund patient not expected to survive without immediate intervention. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 9: Modified Aldrete Score for Post-Sedation Discharge')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Clinical Criteria | Score 2 | Score 1 | Score 0 |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Activity** | Able to move 4 extremities voluntarily or on command | Able to move 2 extremities voluntarily | Unable to move extremities |')
    doc.append('| **Respiration** | Able to breathe deeply and cough freely | Dyspneic, shallow, or limited breathing | Apneic |')
    doc.append('| **Circulation** | Blood pressure $\pm 20\text{ mmHg}$ of pre-sedation level | BP $\pm 20	ext{--}50\text{ mmHg}$ of pre-sedation level | BP $\pm 50\text{ mmHg}$ of pre-sedation level |')
    doc.append('| **Consciousness** | Fully awake | Arousable on calling | Unresponsive |')
    doc.append('| **Oxygen Saturation**| $\text{SpO}_2 > 92\%$ on room air | Requires supplemental $\text{O}_2$ to maintain $\text{SpO}_2 > 90\%$ | $\text{SpO}_2 < 90\%$ despite supplemental $\text{O}_2$ |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('*Total score ranges from 0 to 10; a score of $\ge 9$ is required for discharge from the recovery unit.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 2: Probe Manipulation, Controls & Imaging Planes
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 2. Probe Manipulation, Controls and Multiplane Imaging Planes</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Standardized Probe Maneuvers')
    doc.append('Precise, reproducible TEE imaging requires coordinating six mechanical probe movements and electronic multiplane steering:')
    doc.append('1. **Advance / Withdraw:** Linear movement of the probe deeper into the esophagus/stomach or higher toward the hypopharynx.')
    doc.append('2. **Turn Right / Turn Left:** Axial rotation of the probe shaft clockwise (rightward) or counterclockwise (leftward).')
    doc.append('3. **Anteflex / Retroflex:** Movement of the probe tip anteriorly (toward the anterior chest wall) or posteriorly using the large control wheel.')
    doc.append('4. **Flex Left / Flex Right:** Lateral movement of the probe tip to the patient\'s left or right using the small control wheel.')
    doc.append('5. **Multiplane Angle Rotation:** Electronic steering of the acoustic crystal array from $0^\circ$ (horizontal/transverse) through $90^\circ$ (longitudinal/vertical) to $180^\circ$ (mirror-image transverse).')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-01"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 1: Terminology used to describe TEE probe manipulation](images/fig01_probe_manipulation_terminology.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 1: Standardized terminology used to describe manipulation of the transesophageal echocardiographic transducer: (A) advance and withdraw, (B) rotate right (clockwise) and rotate left (counterclockwise), (C) anteflexion and retroflexion, and (D) lateral flexion right and left.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-02"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 2: Conventions of 2D TEE and multiplane imaging](images/fig02_2d_tee_conventions_views.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 2: Conventions of 2D transesophageal echocardiography. Transducer depth levels (upper esophageal, midesophageal, transgastric, and deep transgastric) and multiplane angle icon ($0^\circ	ext{--}180^\circ$). The apex of the sector is displayed at the top of the screen (closest to the probe).*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-03"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 3: Multiplane biplane imaging and orthogonal cross-sections](images/fig03_multiplane_views_28_cross_sections.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 3: Simultaneous multiplane transesophageal echocardiographic imaging demonstrating primary display plane and simultaneous orthogonal reference plane ($90^\circ$ offset), enabling comprehensive spatial assessment.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

